# What Is Light?

We usually think of light as the thing that helps us see. We switch on a
lamp, look around, and rarely give light another thought. But light does far
more than brighten a room. It carries pictures and voices, helps us take
photographs, gives power to solar panels, and travels to us from the Sun and
distant stars.

The more closely we look at it, the less ordinary it becomes.

What light fundamentally is can seem strange. We say it is a wave and also a
particle. Actually, we use both descriptions to help us understand its
behaviour, because it is difficult to imagine something that is neither a wave
nor a particle. We want everything to be one or the other.

For me, the most satisfying way to think about light is as a disturbance in
the electromagnetic field.

Whoa. What is an electromagnetic field?

Let me put it simply. We have all seen magnets. There is a region around a
magnet where another magnet or a piece of iron can be attracted, or perhaps
repelled if like poles are facing each other. We call this region a magnetic
field: the area around the magnet where we can feel its effect.

Similarly, a charge, such as an electron or a proton, has an electric field
around it. If we place another charge nearby, it will experience a force. Can
you think of an example? Static electricity is a good one. When you rub a comb
through your hair and it attracts small pieces of paper, you are seeing
electric fields in action.

There are trillions of tiny charges around us, inside every material. Electric
and magnetic effects exist at the atomic level. The difference is in what
cancels and what adds together. In a charged comb, the electric charges do not
completely cancel, so we can observe an electric field. In a magnet, the
electric charges may cancel overall, but many tiny magnetic moments line up
and add together, producing a magnetic field.

Now let us come back to light.

If a charge is stationary, its field is steady. But when the charge moves,
especially when it accelerates, it creates a change in the field. That change
can spread outward like a ripple.

It is like a ball disturbing still water and creating ripples that travel
outward. The ball is like the moving charge, and the water is like the field
around it. The important difference is that light does not need water, air, or
any material to travel through. The disturbance can move through empty space.

That travelling disturbance is light.

Different rates of vibration give us different colours. If many colours are
present together, we may see white light. In a sense, white light is a whole
mixture of sounds in the world of light, all arriving together.

How quickly are the ripples of visible light vibrating? They range roughly
from $4 \times 10^{14}$ to $7.5 \times 10^{14}$ vibrations per second. We call
each vibration per second a hertz.

These waves travel through space and interact with almost everything around us.
Every object is made of atoms, and atoms contain charges. Different objects
interact with light in different ways, depending on their texture, shape,
chemical composition, and the colours of light falling on them.

It is remarkable that our eyes and brains have evolved to notice such small
differences. That is what makes vision such a powerful sense.

We have been learning to control light for a very long time. Let us begin at
an interesting point in our history, when light revealed one of its secrets to
us.

We have all seen rainbows. They are beautiful. I wonder what it would feel
like to see one before ever learning what it was. How amazing would that be?

Understanding that these colours could come from white light was one of the
first clues to a world beyond our imagination. It was the first piece of the
puzzle.

I love how we developed curiosity about it, looked more closely, and began to
understand the secrets hidden inside split light.

Let us produce a spectrum of our own. Here, you are seeing almost every colour
that our eyes can detect. The hot surface of the Sun produces light across a
wide range of colours, creating a continuous transition from one colour to
another, with no sudden jumps.

But is that really all there is? I think we should keep zooming in. That is
what scientists did, and that is what we will do too.

## My Own Spectroscope

We started by zooming in on the spectrum we produced. Disappointingly, we
could not see any gaps.

Now let us look at other light sources.

Here is the spectrum from a white LED. We can see the jumps: some colours are
distinct, some are much stronger than others, and there are gaps between them.

And yet the light still looks white. How can that be?

Our eyes do not need every colour to be present in order to see white. A
suitable mixture of red, green, and blue light can appear white to us. This is
the principle behind RGB displays and many modern lights.

An incandescent light works differently. Its filament becomes hot, and the
charges inside it vibrate across a broad range of frequencies, producing a
smooth spread of colours. But there is a catch. It also produces radiation at
frequencies so low that our eyes cannot detect them: infrared light. “Infra”
means below, or beyond the red end of the visible range.

Infrared is invisible to us, but it is not useless. It is used in remote
controls, thermal cameras, sensors, and astronomy. An incandescent bulb
produces more infrared radiation than visible light, which is why it uses so
much electricity to produce the light we want.

We have another source of light: the sodium-vapour lamp.

At first, it may seem strange that different light sources produce different
colours and different patterns. What decides which frequencies can be
produced?

Think about a tuning fork. If you strike it, it vibrates at a particular
frequency. That frequency depends on its mass, length, shape, and elasticity.
You cannot make one tuning fork vibrate at just any frequency you choose. It
has its own natural ways of vibrating.

Atoms behave in a similar way. The electrons inside an atom can only have
certain amounts of energy. When an electron moves from a higher energy level
to a lower one, the atom releases light with a particular frequency. These
released frequencies appear as bright lines in a spectrum.

This is why sodium produces its characteristic yellow lines. Those lines are
like fingerprints. They tell us something about the atoms inside the light
source.

If light passes through a cooler gas, the atoms can absorb particular
frequencies. Those missing frequencies appear as dark lines. Bright lines from
light being released are called an emission spectrum; dark lines caused by
light being absorbed are called an absorption spectrum.

Since we are talking about waves travelling through space, let me introduce
one more idea: the Doppler effect. We will put a pin in it and come back to it
shortly.

You may have heard the changing pitch of an approaching ambulance. As it comes
towards you, the sound waves arrive more closely together and the pitch seems
higher. As it moves away, the waves spread out and the pitch seems lower.

Light can show a similar shift. When the dark lines in a star's spectrum move
towards the red end, we call it a redshift. When they move towards the blue
end, we call it a blueshift.

Now can you connect the dots?

If the familiar pattern of lines has shifted towards red, the source is moving
away from us. When astronomers found that light from distant galaxies was
generally redshifted, they discovered that the galaxies were moving away. The
farther away a galaxy was, the greater its redshift appeared to be, which
meant it was moving away faster.

This observation became one of the main pieces of evidence for the expansion
of the universe and, ultimately, for the Big Bang theory.

